#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100684 :question/id #uuid "fe56a988-49e9-5f33-b0a5-f43d33984c1d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4372" :question/problem "Find the entry $b_{22}$ of the matrix ${B},$ where \n\n$$\n{B}\n=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The entry $b_{22}$ lies at the intersection of the $2$nd row and $2$nd column. Therefore, $b_{22}={\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100686 :question/id #uuid "0928c2fa-a772-5d39-a638-1b1d0e71be2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4378" :question/problem "Calculate $A-(B-C),$ where\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Performing the operations on each corresponding entry, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(B-C) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1-0 & -1-(-5) \\\\ 2-1 & 0-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1-(-1) & 0-4 \\\\ 2-1 & -2-(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f88e9a5-8759-5e71-b262-00b079bef50f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388343} {:answer/id #uuid "f2609425-f61f-5689-a1b2-ad1766ba4f2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388344} {:answer/id #uuid "065fbb44-49f9-5677-ae45-0db8eae4e9ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388345} {:answer/id #uuid "6d7d6d9c-e6a7-5790-92dd-e8b2bca71761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388346} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fb0aa4-9a2d-5c94-a1be-3f68d1da0be1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388346} :answer-field/id #uuid "aefbe9b4-2591-5f99-bd99-dce5ef9f6719" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388342}] :question/id #uuid "60ff0898-484c-5468-ab70-2a2b8951960b" :question/math-academy-id "q-13536" :question/problem "Find $(A+B)-C-(B-C)$, if\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)-C-(B-C) & =A+B-C-B+C \\\\  & =A+\\cancel{B}-\\cancel{C}-\\cancel{B}+\\cancel{C} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single matrix. So, the result is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ce47e15-8a71-5a0e-877b-8161ee17ff71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388418} {:answer/id #uuid "84d4840e-ffea-5fc0-bd15-47e2b8b3ee8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388419} {:answer/id #uuid "a052459a-e452-5bfb-9115-9b9d15dcfb91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388420} {:answer/id #uuid "b4829af7-c55a-5072-9da4-2384966bfee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388421} {:answer/id #uuid "f605914a-ff93-5823-885b-2509f3adee21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388422} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef949d1a-3425-5215-bdb0-047bedfe1e0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388417}] :question/id #uuid "c6f60840-c1ce-5025-8b9b-ed8f00fe0d5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-13564" :question/problem "Find $Ax$ if \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-6)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 4 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8\\cdot 2+(-5)\\cdot 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$Ax=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3819a4e-2124-51a9-966d-a232f795727b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388506} {:answer/id #uuid "e5497e19-b13b-5352-a486-89ae75f2fe36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388507} {:answer/id #uuid "1524532d-303c-57e8-baaa-e00f625a908a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388508} {:answer/id #uuid "f505e5ac-8344-5745-ab05-6ca6bf802e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The product is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388509} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6230bd-503c-59b4-8461-692f556f4ea7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388507} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bf67a40-3ab5-50a6-9561-e2ee6d875a8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388505}] :question/id #uuid "612ed9a2-f644-5c99-b797-cab8e5ee69a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-13762" :question/problem "Calculate the product ${I}_{2}B$ given that ${I}_{2}$ is the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix and\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix ${I}_{2}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\n{I}_{2}\\cdot B=B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e77b0e35-b8ea-5618-ab6c-8503315df0e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3.5 \\\\ -2.5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392443} {:answer/id #uuid "b90bbe8b-d8e5-5080-9aa2-4142743a4b96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392444} {:answer/id #uuid "6b52aa96-4f70-58ea-b2cf-28f779b7b43b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2\\sqrt{5} & -2.5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392445} {:answer/id #uuid "d06d67b6-8acb-58a8-957c-d55d012736a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392446} {:answer/id #uuid "e47cb90f-8189-59b3-96b0-469513eb54fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} & -3 & 3.5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392444} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cb1f592-094a-5b91-bac8-80971fe96ac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392442}] :question/id #uuid "bde0cdd0-7dbe-5ba4-9b36-e66cfe099501" :question/math-academy-id "q-41949" :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} & -3 & 3.5 \\\\ -2 & 2\\sqrt{5} & -2.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ is the $1$st *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ by the $1$st *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3},$ the result must be the $1$st *column* of $A.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} & -3 & 3.5 \\\\ -2 & 2\\sqrt{5} & -2.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 0+3.5\\cdot 0 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot 1+2\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 0+(-2.5)\\cdot 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101952 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the following matrix:\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nWe denote the entry that lies at the intersection of the $\\color{blue}3$rd row and $\\color{red}2$nd column using the notation $a_{{\\color{blue}3}\\color{red}2}.$\n\nThe first subscript represents the row and the second subscript represents the column. So $a_{{\\color{blue}3}\\color{red}2}=8,$ as shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & \\boxed{8} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can denote every entry of a matrix in this way.\nFor instance, $a_{{\\color{blue}4}\\color{red}1}$ denotes the entry that lies at the intersection of the $\\color{blue}4$th row and $\\color{red}1$st column.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & {a}_{13} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & {a}_{23} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32} & {a}_{33} \\\\ {a}_{41} & {a}_{42} & {a}_{43}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "34f3a63d-a273-50fb-b26a-ae172c7e93e9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2969 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101954 :tutorial/content "If we want to add (or subtract) three or more matrices, all operations should be done elementwise. For example, suppose\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nThen we can calculate ${A}+{B}-{C}$ by performing the operations on each corresponding entry, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B-C & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+(-1)-0 \\\\ 1+2-5 \\\\ -1+0-(-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f6d0bdee-d80b-596a-bef2-41b2d6429eeb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2973 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101959 :tutorial/content "When multiplying two matrices in general, we apply the same approach as usual:\n\n- First, we verify that the two matrices are conformable for multiplication.\n\n- Then, we multiply each row in the first matrix by each column in the second.\n\nFor example, let's find $AB,$ where \n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFirst, we verify the conformability for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{3}}}{A} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\cdot} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{3}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{B} = \n\t\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{C}\n$$\n\nSo the matrices are conformable for multiplication, and the product is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix. Let's set up the multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        -2 & 2 & 1  \\\\\n\t1 & -1 & 3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\n\n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        -5 & 2   \\\\\n\t2 & -2   \\\\\n\t-4 & 1 \n    \\end{bmatrix}}_{B}\n\n\t&= \n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\ast & \\ast   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\t}_{C}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the entries of the product matrix (shown as $\\ast$'s in the equation above).\n\nTo find $c_{11},$ the entry at the intersection of the $1$st row and $1$st column, we calculate the dot product of the $1$st row of $A$ and the $1$st column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{blue}-2 & \\color{blue}2 & \\color{blue}1  \\\\\n\t\\color{lightgray}1 & \\color{lightgray}-1 & \\color{lightgray}3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n         \\color{red}-5 & \\color{lightgray}2   \\\\\n\t\\color{red}2 & \\color{lightgray}-2   \\\\\n\t\\color{red}-4 & \\color{lightgray}1\n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n%\t&= \n%    \\left[\n%   \\begin{matrix}\n%       ({\\color{blue}{-2}}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}}) + {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-4}}) & \\ast   \\\\\n%        \\ast & \\ast\n%    \\end{matrix}\n%    \\right]\n\n\t= \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{10} & \\ast   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{bmatrix}, && \\quad \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{10} = ({\\color{blue}{-2}}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}}) + {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-4}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{12},$ we calculate the dot product of the $1$st row of $A$ and the $2$nd column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{blue}-2 & \\color{blue}2 & \\color{blue}1  \\\\\n\t\\color{lightgray}1 & \\color{lightgray}-1 & \\color{lightgray}3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{lightgray}-5 & \\color{red}2   \\\\\n\t\\color{lightgray}2 & \\color{red}-2   \\\\\n\t\\color{lightgray}-4 & \\color{red}1\n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n%\t&= \n%    \\left[\n%    \\begin{matrix}\n%        10 & ({\\color{blue}{-2}}) \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}}) + {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}   \\\\\n%        \\ast & \\ast\n%    \\end{matrix}\n%    \\right]\n\n\t= \\begin{bmatrix}\n        10 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-7}   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{bmatrix}, && \\quad \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-7} = ({\\color{blue}{-2}}) \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}}) + {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{21},$ we calculate the dot product of the $2$nd row of $A$ and the $1$st column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{lightgray}-2 & \\color{lightgray}2 & \\color{lightgray}1  \\\\\n\t\\color{blue}1 & \\color{blue}-1 & \\color{blue}3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n         \\color{red}-5 & \\color{lightgray}2   \\\\\n\t\\color{red}2 & \\color{lightgray}-2   \\\\\n\t\\color{red}-4 & \\color{lightgray}1\n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n%\t&= \n%    \\left[\n%    \\begin{matrix}\n%        10 & -7   \\\\\n%        {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}}) + ({\\color{blue}{-1}}) \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-4}}) & \\ast\n%    \\end{matrix}\n%    \\right]\n\n\t= \\begin{bmatrix}\n        10 & -7   \\\\\n        \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-19} & \\ast\n    \\end{bmatrix}, && \\quad \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-19} = {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}}) + ({\\color{blue}{-1}}) \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-4}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find $c_{22},$ we calculate the dot product of the $2$nd row of $A$ and the $2$nd column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{lightgray}-2 & \\color{lightgray}2 & \\color{lightgray}1  \\\\\n\t\\color{blue}1 & \\color{blue}-1 & \\color{blue}3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{lightgray}-5 & \\color{red}2   \\\\\n\t\\color{lightgray}2 & \\color{red}-2   \\\\\n\t\\color{lightgray}-4 & \\color{red}1\n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n%\t&= \n%    \\left[\n%    \\begin{matrix}\n%        10 & -7   \\\\\n%        -19 & {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + ({\\color{blue}{-1}}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}}) + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}\n%    \\end{matrix}\n%    \\right]\n\n\t= \\begin{bmatrix}\n        10 & -7   \\\\\n        -19 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{7}\n    \\end{bmatrix}, && \\quad \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{7} = {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + ({\\color{blue}{-1}}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}}) + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -7 \\\\ -19 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ce402944-0109-5d9d-93d5-394323fbc469" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2993 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101961 :tutorial/content "Remember that the **identity matrix** $I_n$ is an $n \\times n$ square matrix with $1$'s on the diagonal, and where all other elements are $0.$ For example, the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix $I_3$ is given below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe identity matrix gets its name from the fact that whenever we multiply a matrix by the corresponding identity matrix, the matrix remains unchanged.\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nMultiplying the matrix $A$ by the identity matrix $I_3,$ we find that the result is just $A$ itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot {I}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 0 & (-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+10\\cdot 0 & (-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 1 \\\\ 6\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 0 & 6\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0 & 6\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1 \\\\ 23\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0+8\\cdot 0 & 23\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 0 & 23\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 0+8\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A \\cdot I_3 = A.$ You can check that $I_3 \\cdot A = A$ using the same method.\n\nThis result holds in general: \n\n> For *any* square $n \\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n> \n> \n> $$\n> A \\cdot I_n = I_n \\cdot A = A.\n>\n$$\n\nIn other words, the $n \\times n$ identity matrix $I_n$ has a similar property to the number $1,$ namely that \n\n$$\na\\cdot 1 = 1\\cdot a = a\n$$\n\n for any real number $a.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5383efb1-6e51-562a-bf79-9ab8cba87ed7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2995 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101962 :tutorial/content "Whenever we multiply a matrix $A$ by the $n$th column of the identity matrix, the result is the $n$th column of $A.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nThe first column of the identity matrix $I_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf we multiply $A$ by the column on the *right*, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product is just the first column of the matrix $A.$\n\nUsing the same method, you can check for yourself that when we multiply $A$ by the second column of $I_2,$ the result is the second column of $A.$\n\nOn the other hand, if we multiply $A$ by the first *row* of the identity matrix $I_2$ on the *left*, we will obtain the first row of $A$. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2c46b879-fe8a-5f8e-8579-71b1bc92ac07" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2996 :tutorial/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column of the Identity Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101963 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have two vectors $\\textbf{u}=\\langle 1, 3 \\rangle$ and $\\textbf{v}=\\langle 2,-1 \\rangle.$ The parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}$ is shown in the picture below:\n\n![](images/10/102f72ba6730d1b07ca0d01186a1ff7851954193f00dc74eb7150981844b0222.png)\n\nNow, we construct a new matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$M =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nwhere the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors. Let's find the determinant of $M\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt turns out that the absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|det(M)| \\\\  & =|-7| \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "90a7caf1-54f7-517e-93cb-98a6d39494ad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3014 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101964 :tutorial/content "Why does the absolute value of the determinant represent the area of the parallelogram? Let's prove this result.\n\nFirst, let's state the result precisely. Consider two vectors $\\textbf{u}=\\langle a,b \\rangle$ and $\\textbf{v}=\\langle c,d \\rangle.$ The parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}$ is shown in the picture below:\n\n![](images/3a/3a466fd3c52ea345d80b9ba67ac104efef29bfd7eeed0e4567364c80f3212c0d.png)\n\nWe construct a new matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$M =\\begin{bmatrix}a & c \\\\ b & d\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nwhere the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors. Then the absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nA = |\\det(M)|\n$$\n\nTo prove the formula, we use the following picture:\n\n![](images/3d/3da110b114d2038738bd4ef06cb031bb8b66aee062ffc83955dfdaade72584e1.png)\n\nWe can compute the area by starting with the area of the outer rectangle, and then subtracting off the area of the smaller triangles and rectangles surrounding the parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|(a+c)(b+d)-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}cd-2\\cdot ad-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}ab| \\\\  & =|ab+ad+bc+cd-cd-2ad-ab| \\\\  & =|bc-ad| \\\\  & =|ad-bc| \\\\  & =|det(M)|\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ca25b302-10d5-5d18-a10f-0c50af28db94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3015 :tutorial/title "General Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101966 :tutorial/content "When two vectors are parallel, the parallelogram does not exist. So, it has zero area.\n\n![](images/b6/b65c64ccf16764f99c0c30dbf6b37ce7b6254b83496512a5d207dddab095dac7.png)\n\nAs a result, the determinant of a matrix whose columns are parallel vectors must also be zero. \n\nFor example, in the diagram above, we have the vectors $\\textbf{u} = \\left< 1,1 \\right>$ and $\\textbf{v} = \\left< 2,2 \\right>.$ Constructing a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors, we have\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$M =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nIndeed, the determinant does come out to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ff830819-9cbf-5fb9-852c-f885f8cd6d68" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3173 :tutorial/title "The Case When Two Vectors are Parallel"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106035 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e0d6486-d718-528b-bd33-68b4cd544cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $10$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396006} {:answer/id #uuid "2ec8852b-bfa4-53a1-8fe2-e8c75b78fa64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396007} {:answer/id #uuid "8c271403-1e23-550b-9359-7ec7a8d9dd0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396008} {:answer/id #uuid "1e78af7e-059a-524f-9ce9-77e30b3e4934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $10$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396009} {:answer/id #uuid "db26a554-6026-5b02-9aa7-d6f9e0ad3b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396008} :answer-field/id #uuid "e17cbcad-7731-506c-bd2a-44566af3416a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396005}] :question/id #uuid "6ec67f6b-b339-5863-9fd2-be6322d61819" :question/math-academy-id "q-12044" :question/problem "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of  $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=i \\\\ T(j) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=10i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a *horizontal* shear with a shear factor of $10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e72b8eba-d97d-557f-a8cb-b177b5a694e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401317} {:answer/id #uuid "239808c3-6662-5144-b216-bf28876d8c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401318} {:answer/id #uuid "d95c8151-cc29-5467-9bd0-4f7145939834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401319} {:answer/id #uuid "a539e88c-cfb9-5231-b8f6-481fa85848fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401320} {:answer/id #uuid "ace8a67a-e74f-5f27-9582-9a2b3428a829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401317} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dd41be4-e0dd-5cb0-830a-bc68816c31ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401316}] :question/id #uuid "ba678a53-c013-5d44-b391-2d5cc0a9cc96" :question/math-academy-id "q-162150" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c397e37-1d42-5efe-81d5-6ff323b2d323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394052} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6ed60d-2840-53c0-a19c-73a1c38e3ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394053} {:answer/id #uuid "38883822-19a8-50c5-ac70-21dd818b00e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394054} {:answer/id #uuid "75955132-8432-5b46-8ff2-1f131bf31dd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394055} {:answer/id #uuid "48d10010-6d30-5ed7-a764-671a0140e320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394053} :answer-field/id #uuid "78374675-6ad5-5e77-a243-9ee8ff3a2a6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394051}] :question/id #uuid "85f5eecb-79b7-5107-8347-e7d15fcb457b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80726" :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v)=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $v$ and $T(v),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-component of $T(v)$ is $2$ times the $x$-component of $v,$ while\n\n- the $y$-components of $v$ and $T(v)$ are the same.\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $2$ in the $x$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107088 :tutorial/content "Consider the standard basis $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\big\\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors.\n\nLet $$\n{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ be a matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf T.$ We can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=2i \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this transformation, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/61/61fc1f3f1e76ff49b9967399d1b3a3a63bbe9845a59f2b2841af6e14510b2a7a.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation represented by the matrix $T$ multiplies the vector $\\mathbf{i}$ by a factor of $2$ and leaves the vector $\\mathbf j$ unchanged. This type of transformation is called a **stretch in the $\\mathbf x$-direction.** We can also call it a **horizontal stretch.** In this case, we have a **horizontal stretch of scale factor** $\\mathbf 2.$\n\nIn general, a stretch in the $x$-direction of scale factor $k$ with its center at the origin is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2b251d5e-1184-5f44-b4ff-ba8c7a4fa35d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3538 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107089 :tutorial/content "Consider the standard basis $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\big\\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors.\n\nLet $$\n{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ be a matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf T.$ We can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=i \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0.5i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this transformation, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/aa/aa646019f6af73e795661060a1a678b5b3be7961d7e60e3ae601d911033382d2.png)\n\nThe transformation represented by the matrix $T$ adds $0.5\\mathbf{i}$ to $\\mathbf j,$ while the vector $\\mathbf i$ remains unchanged. This type of transformation is called a **horizontal shear.** In this case, we have a **horizontal shear with a shear factor of** $\\mathbf{0.5}.$\n\nIn general, a horizontal shear of scale factor $k$ with its center at the origin is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4c09cece-483e-5612-b070-e22441b1fce3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3541 :tutorial/title "Horizontal Shear"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107101 :tutorial/content "Now, let <div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div> be a matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf T.$ We can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=i \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this transformation, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/19/19b1f75508ae7fc0255edcf2821bb26df352f46544add7740957291d9cb8e117.png)\n\nThe transformation represented by the matrix $T$ multiplies the vector $\\mathbf{j}$ by $2$ and leaves the vector $\\mathbf i$ unchanged. This type of transformation is called a **stretch in the $\\mathbf y$-direction.** We can also call this a **vertical stretch**. In this case, we have a **vertical stretch of scale factor** $\\mathbf 2$. \n\nIn general, a stretch in the $y$-direction of scale factor $k$ with its center at the origin is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1e893b6a-17a2-5f2b-8a94-e7237e811fe5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4414 :tutorial/title "Vertical Stretch"}]}
